  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chilly overnight period Tuesday night. Slight \nchance for frost in the north. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nCool airmass moves over northern Maine Tuesday night. There is \na chance for atmospheric decoupling early in the night, as skies\nclear out. This will help temperatures to drop into the 30s. \nPotential for northern areas in northern Aroostook to drop into \nthe low-to-mid 30s, which could lead to some frost development. \nFrost chances are going to be highly dependent on the timing of \nsome high level clouds moving in late Tuesday night into early \nWednesday morning. If the clouds move in earlier in the night, \ntemperatures will stabilize faster. If clouds move in slower, \nthen there is more time for the temperatures to drop with clear \nskies.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances remain limited through midweek, \naccompanied by near to below normal temperatures. The next best \nchance for precipitation arrives Wednesday night into Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system begins to approach from the northwest in \nCanada, tracking eastward and passing over Maine Wednesday night\ninto Thursday. Rain moves in from the west Wednesday night, and\ncontinues to track east until it leaves the region Thursday \nnight. Global models are predicting PWATs around 1.8-1.9 inches,\nwhich would be well over the 90th percentile and potentially \nover the daily max PWAT record in Caribou. There will be plenty \nof dry air that this system will need to overcome Wednesday \nnight. Models hinting at generally a widespread 0.5 inches of \nrainfall, with a chance for isolated areas of higher rainfall. \n\nNot anticipating much in terms of convection with this rain \nsystem, with relatively low CAPE, shear, and lapse rates. Chance\nfor a low level jet to move over the region along with the \nassociated cold front, which could bring gusty winds along the \nDowneast coast and over the waters. Warm sector ahead of the \nfront brings warmer temperatures into the Central Highlands, \nBangor, and Downeast regions on Thursday, in the upper-70s to \nlow-80s.",
